# Defect Healing in FAPb(I1‐xBrx)3 Perovskites: Multifunctional Fluorinated Sulfonate Surfactant Anchoring Enables >21% Modules with Improved Operation Stability

## abstract
Formamidinium lead triiodide‐based perovskite solar cells have emerged as one of the most promising candidates that can be potentially used to develop photovoltaic technologies in the future. The commercial use of perovskite solar cell modules (PSCMs) is limited as it is challenging to fabricate high‐quality, efficient, and stable large‐area perovskite light‐absorbing films. Heptadecafluorooctanesulfonic acid tetraethylammonium salt (HFSTT), containing fluorinated long alkyl chains as hydrophobic tails and sulfonic acid groups (SO3−) as hydrophilic heads, which exhibit a great synergistic potential in large‐area film uniform fabrication, crystallization orientation modulation, defect passivation, and device operation stability enhancement, are introduced. The HFSTT‐modified films exhibit a prominent (100) orientation and lower trap‐state density as well as enhanced carrier mobilities and diffusion lengths, facilitating a champion unit device with an impressive power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 23.88% (0.14 cm2) and 22.52% (1 cm2) with a low voltage deficit around 0.341 V. The unencapsulated device retains ≈70% of its initial efficiency after 1000 h under heat damping test (60 °C and ≈60% RH). Moreover, the PSCMs exhibiting PCEs of 21.05% (with notable fill factor 0.79) and 18.27% are characterized by the active areas of 25.98 and 60.68 cm2, respectively.

Defect Healing in FAPb(I $|_{\neg{\boldsymbol{x}}}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\rangle_{3}$ Perovskites: Multifunctional Fluorinated Sulfonate Surfactant Anchoring Enables >21% Modules with Improved Operation Stability
Formamidinium lead triiodide-based perovskite solar cells have emerged as one of the most promising candidates that can be potentially used to develop photovoltaic technologies in the future. The commercial use of perovskite solar cell modules (PSCMs) is limited as it is challenging to fabricate high-quality, efficient, and stable large-area perovskite light-absorbing films. Heptadecafluorooctanesulfonic acid tetraethylammonium salt (HFSTT), containing fluorinated long alkyl chains as hydrophobic tails and sulfonic acid groups (SO3−) as hydrophilic heads, which exhibit a great synergistic potential in large-area film uniform fabrication, crystallization orientation modulation, defect passivation, and device operation stability enhancement, are introduced. The HFSTTmodified films exhibit a prominent (100) orientation and lower trap-state density as well as enhanced carrier mobilities and diffusion lengths, facilitating a champion unit device with an impressive power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $23.88\%(0.74\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and $22.52\%(]\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ with a low voltage deficit around 0.341 V. The unencapsulated device retains $\approx70\%$ of its initial efficiency after $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{1000}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ under heat damping test $(60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $\approx60\%$ RH). Moreover, the PSCMs exhibiting PCEs of $21.05\%$ (with notable fill factor 0.79) and $18.27\%$ are characterized by the active areas of 25.98 and $60.68~\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ , respectively.
## 1. Introduction
Metal halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are one of the most promising optoelectronic active layers for upcoming photovoltaic technologies owing to its excellent photoelectric properties (such as ambipolar charge mobility,[1,2] low exciton binding energy,[3,4] strong high absorption coefficients,[5] tunable band gaps,[6,7] and long carrier diffusion lengths[8,9]). Labscale PSC-based devices with an active area of $\scriptstyle\approx0.1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ exhibit excellent performances and present outstanding certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) values of $525\%$ .[10–12]  However, the most urgent issue remained to be solved is the huge gap in operation stability form lab-scale to commercialization.[13–15] Although, the small-area PSCs devices have shown a great progress in stability enhancement,[16–18] there are few reports on studies focusing on improving the longterm stabilities of perovskite solar cell modules (PSCMs). Qi and colleagues recently adopted a holistic stabilization approach to improve the stability of the interfaces in minimodules.[19] A defect compensation strategy was developed to improve the stabilities of minimodules by suppressing the extent of ion migration achieved and reducing the number of defects generated under conditions of long-term illumination.[20] Nevertheless, upscaling of lab perovskite solar cells to module scale with improved intrinsic stability and minimal efficiency loss is still missing.
The perovskite absorbing layer compositions play a significant role in the fabrication of highly efficient and stable devices. Most of the highly efficient PSCs contain methylammonium cations $(\mathrm{MA^{+}})$ .[21–24] The low stability of the $\mathrm{MA^{+}}$ mixed perovskites can be attributed to the volatile nature of the system and the property of cation migration.[25,26] It has also been reported that the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ system is characterized by low phase segregation and decomposition temperature. These systems undergo irreversible structural degradation under conditions of light or moisture stress.[27–30] $\mathrm{FAPb}(\mathrm{I}_{1\cdot x}\mathrm{Br}_{x})_{3}$ with mixed-hailed anion perovskites have shown a great pentation in device thermaland phase-stability than methylammonium containing perovskites.[31–36] To date, $\mathrm{FAPb}(\mathrm{I}_{1\cdot x}\mathrm{Br}_{x})_{3}$ -based devices characterized by high PCE and excellent photostability have not been reported.
It would be caused by the nature of solution-processed polycrystalline perovskite film, where the perovskite surface and bulk grain boundaries are important sources for nonradiative recombination, which can heavily trapping photogenerated charge carriers and thus inhibiting the performance of the device.[37] Researchers have extensively used various techniques such as iodide management,[38,39] additive engineering,[40–42] composition engineering,[43,44] grain boundary /surface passivation,[45–47] and nonradiative recombination suppression[48,49] in the past few years to improve the quality of MA-free perovskite films. Studies to simultaneously fabricate uniform, high-quality, and large-area perovskite films with modulated crystallization orientation, annihilated defects, and improved device operation stability have rarely been conducted.
Heptadecafluorooctanesulfonic acid tetraethylammonium salt (HFSTT) was introduced as a multifunctional surfactant. This compound can function as a surface tension regulator, defect passivator, and perovskite structure stabilizer. In our strategy, HFSTT was introduced into the perovskite precursor solution as a multifunctional additive to regulate the surface tension of the perovskite solution and fabricate a high-quality large-area perovskite film. The introduction of HFSTT also helped to significantly passivate the positively or negatively charged defects at the perovskite surfaces and grain boundaries. As a result, the PCEs of the target PSCs containing $0.3\mathrm{\mol}\%$ of HFSTT in small-scale sizes were recorded to be $23.88\%$ $(0.14~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and $22.52\%$ $(1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ . The strategy was successfully extended to fabricate 25.98 and $60.68~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ PSCMs that exhibited excellent performances of $21.05\%$ and $18.27\%$ , respectively. Considering a geometric fill factor (GFF, ${\mathrm{GFF}}= $ active area/ aperture area $=92.5\%$ ), the aperture area efficiencies in these modules were $19.33\%$ and $16.90\%$ , respectively. Notably, the champion module characterized by an active area of $25.98~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ exhibits an excellent fill factor (FF) of $\approx78.7\%$ . Open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ loss from lab-scale samples upscaled to form the module is not observed under these conditions. In addition, the module exhibits excellent operational stability, resulting in the generation of a stabilized PCE of $20.4\%$ over a period of $4000\mathrm{~s~}$ tracking under conditions of continuous light soaking in ambient air (relative humidity (RH) controlled in the range of $\approx5\mathrm{-}10\%$ , temperature of module was maintained at ${\approx}45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ). Furthermore, the epoxy encapsulated minimodules also exhibit impressive long-term light stabilities. Approximately $80\%$ of the initial efficiency was retained when the system was continuously operated under 1 sun illumination over a period of $2000\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2. Results and Discussion
We used an MA-free perovskite of the composition $(\mathrm{FAPbI_{3}})_{0.98}(\mathrm{FAPbBr_{3}})_{0.02}$ as an efficient light-absorbing layer. The film-formation kinetics is one of the crucial parameters that need to be controlled to fabricate uniform films characterized by large areas and low trap density following the singlestep spin-coating process using an antisolvent. A small amount $(0.3\mathrm{\mol\%}$ relative to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) of HFSTT was directly added into the perovskite precursor solution as a surfactant. Generally, the mechanism of nucleation and crystal growth associated with the film formation process (attributable to the evaporation of the solvent) can be explained by the LaMer theory. The process proceeds over three stages: i) solution stage, ii) nucleation and fast-growth stage, and iii) slow-growth stage. The optical microscopy technique was used to investigate the role of HFSTT in the perovskite nucleation and growth process. The perovskite films were deposited on fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) substrates following the spin-coating method, and the optical images were recorded as a function of the drying time (we regard coating program ending time as $t_{0}$ ). Analysis of Figure 1a,b reveals that several crystals were formed in the pristine wet film immediately after reaching $t_{0}$ . In the case of the HFSTT-incorporated film, the crystal advent time was delayed to $t_{0}+10\mathrm{~s~}$ The density of crystals at the moment of crystal formation and the number of crystallites (small and uniform) formed in the wet film doped with HFSTT $(0.3\mathrm{\mol\%})$ were higher than the density and number of crystallites formed in the pristine sample. This indicates that HFSTT inhibits the processes of rapid natural supersaturation, uncontrollable nucleation, and grain growth processes. This can be attributed to the strong interaction between the HFSTT unit and the perovskite precursor.
Due to the high surface tension and nonvolatile property of perovskite solvents (dimethylformamide and dimethyl sulfoxide),[50] it is a challenge to fabricate a large perovskite layer with compact and uniform perovskite film via one step spincoating method, since perovskite precursor solution is difficult to fully adhere on the rough FTO surface via a high-speed spin coating method and thus impossible to fabricate a uniform and compact perovskite film on a large scale $(10\times10~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ . The introduction of the HFSTT surfactant, containing fluorinated long alkyl chains (hydrophobic tails) and sulfonic acid groups $\left(\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}\right.$ ; hydrophilic heads), can help regulate the surface tension of the perovskite solution (Figure 1c(1, 4)). As shown in Figure S1 of the Supporting Information, the perovskite precursor solution contact angle decreased from $21.4^{\circ}$ to $12.7^{\circ}$ . The formation of such compact films was also confirmed by analyzing the cross-sectional scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The HFSTT-treated perovskite film fits tightly on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface, and the presence of a large number of interfacial voids are observed at the bottom interface of the pristine film.[51]
It has been proved that the perovskite precursor solutions are colloidal dispersions.[52,53] In the pristine solution, the colloidal precursor, $\mathrm{FA^{+}{-}P b I}_{x-}$ complex, is formed spontaneously in the precursor solution and the nucleation and growth stages of the perovskite crystallites are not distinct. This results in the formation of crystallites characterized by uncontrolled size (Figure 1c (2)). The dynamic light scattering (DLS) technique was used to determine the size distribution of the colloidal particles in the precursor solutions. As shown in Figure S3 of the Supporting Information, the DLS profiles recorded for the pristine solution reveal the presence of two peaks at 20 and $428~\mathrm{nm}$ , whereas the profile recorded for the HFSTT-doped precursor solution reveals the presence of one peak of $92~\mathrm{nm}$ . The average size of the particles reveals that the HFSTT-introduced solution is characterized by a homogenous size distribution of colloids. The introduction of HFSTT into the precursor solution can potentially help decouple the nucleation and growth stages associated with the formation of perovskite crystallites. The electrostatic interaction between the long-chain surfactant and colloids can hinder the aggregation of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ nanoclusters. It can also help in aligning the nanocluster and forming an ordered structure (Figure 1c (5)). We hypothesized that the HFSTT molecules could get anchored on the surface of the perovskite nucleus, it could result in the suppression of the formation of the undesired $\delta$ phase $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ and hinder the tilting of the grains during growth. Eventually, a (100)-orientation-dominant film with low defect density (lower than the density of the randomly oriented pristine film) could be obtained (Figure $\textstyle1\mathbf{c}(3,6))$ . As confirmed by the grazing incidence X-ray diffraction technique. The results presented in Figure 1d,e revealed that compared to the pristine perovskite film, the HFSTT-modified film exhibited more than ten times higher (100) peak intensity. The modified film helped in significantly suppressing the formation of the $\delta$ phase $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ . During the growth of the sample, the fluorinated long alkyl chain-containing hydrophobic HFSTT additives are expelled, and they gather on the surface of the perovskite film or fill the grain boundaries. This is indicated by the water-contact-angle measurements (Figure S4, Supporting Information). Which is consist with previous work, where the fluorine-contain additives is beneficial to crystallize and enable to improve film quality and device stability.[54–56]
We further used the top-view SEM to monitor the effect of HFSTT on the morphology of the perovskite film. Analysis of Figure 2a,e reveals that the pristine sample is characterized by a mixed phase of white $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and big black perovskite grains.[57] Following the incorporation of HFSTT, the white $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ phase disappeared, and a dense and large-grained film was formed. The formation of the film could be attributed to the strong coordination between the HFSTT and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ units that resulted in a delay in the process of the perovskite nucleation. This helped in suppressing the content of unreacted $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . The crystallization of the perovskite was also promoted under these conditions (Figure  1e; the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ phase disappeared when HFSTT was incorporated into the system). The corresponding statistical distribution recorded for the grain size reveals that the average grain size of the HFSTT-modified sample is $1065\pm$ $250~\mathrm{nm}$ . The average grain size is $713\pm150\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the pristine sample (Figure  2b,f; Figure S5, Supporting Information). The results agree well with the results obtained by analyzing the cross-sectional SEM images recorded for the planar structure, where the HFSTT perovskite film is characterized by large and dense grains and few grain boundaries (Figure 2c,g). The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) techniques were used to understand the nature of the interaction between the HFSTT units and the perovskite materials. The stretching vibration peaks corresponding to the $\mathrm{SO_{3}}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{2}$ groups in HFSTT were observed at 1262.8 and $1202.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure  2d). The peaks corresponding to the stretching vibrations of the $\mathrm{SO^{3-}}$ and ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{2}$ groups in HFSTT-incorporated perovskite film were observed at 1272.3 and $1210\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The shift in the peak positions can be attributed to the coordination of the HFSTT with the perovskite. The $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{S}}=0$ group donates the unpaired electrons to the empty orbitals of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and chelates with the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions as well.[58] In addition, the shift in the XPS spectral profile recorded for the perovskite incorporated with HFSTT further confirms the coordination between the HFSTT unit and the $\mathrm{Pb}$ ions (Figure  2h). The peaks at $142.76\$ $\ensuremath{(\mathrm{Pb~}4\mathrm{f}_{7/2})}$ and 137.91 (Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\rangle$ ) eV shifted toward low binding energy regions (142.46 and 137.61  eV, respectively) following the introduction of the HFSTT unit. This could be attributed to the effect of defect passivation and electron donation (from the oxygen of the $\mathrm{SO}_{3}{}^{-}$ group).[59,60] Figure S6 of the Supporting Information shows the I 3d XPS spectra, where the 3d5/2 and the 3d3/2 peaks are located at 618.95 and $630.43\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the reference sample, which is attributed to the Pb–I chemical species. The HFSTT-modified sample shifts the doublet to lower binding energies at $618.55$ and $630.03\ \mathrm{~eV}.$ The shift of I 3d peaks to lower binding energy indicates that the oxidation state of iodide is decreased or the $\mathrm{\sfPb\mathrm{-}I}$ bond is weakened by interaction with the tetraethylammonium cations. The results obtained using the XPS technique agreed well with the results obtained using the FTIR technique. This indicated the presence of strong coordination interactions between the HFSTT units and the Pb ions. We propose that the HFSTT molecules can neutralize the charge defects at the surfaces and grain boundaries of the perovskite film as they bear positively and negatively charged components (Figure 2i). The high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) technique was used to further confirm the grain boundary passivation effect generated in the presence of HFSTT. The HRTEM technique was primarily used to analyze the grain boundary regions under conditions of high magnification. Analysis of the images reveals that the HFSTT units fill the grain boundaries in the perovskite films. The pristine film presents no spatial gap between each grain (Figure 2j), whereas the film containing HFSTT presents amorphous gaps between the grains (Figure 2k). This could be attributed to the expulsion of HFSTT from the perovskite grains. The expulsion could be attributed to the hydrophobic nature of the HFSTT tail.[61] The added of HFSTT in perovskite is primarily passivating the perovskite grain boundary and providing a beneficial barrier against moisture and diffusion of ions, since the grain boundaries contains abundant wrong or dangling bonds, which serve as the carrier recombination center and first sites of perovskite decomposition. Ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) absorption and photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy techniques were used to evaluate the quality and charge carrier behavior of the perovskite film. It was observed that the incorporation of HFSTT did not affect the nature of the UV–vis absorption spectral profile over $550~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  2l). This indicated that HFSTT does not affect the bandgap of the perovskite system. However, the steady-state PL profile presented in Figure  2m shows a 2.45-fold enhanced intensity without any peak shift. This indicates that the nonradiative recombination process occurring within the HFSTT-added perovskite film is significantly suppressed. Time-resolved PL (TRPL) significantly promotes the process of retarded charge-recombination under conditions of HFSTT doping (Figure 2n). The TRPL data were fitted to a biexponential decay equation (see fitting results in Table S1, Supporting Information) and the average lifetimes increased from 304.92 ns (pristine) to 735.76 ns (perovskite with HFSTT).
We used PSCs with an n–i–p-based planar heterojunction architecture and an active area of $0.14\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ $\mathrm{(FTO/TiO_{2}/}$ $(\mathrm{FAPbI_{3}})_{0.98}(\mathrm{FAPbBr_{3}})_{0.02}/\mathrm{\Omega}$ Spiro-OMeTAD/Au) to investigate the effect of HFSTT modification on the photovoltaic performance. Figure 3a presents the statistics of the photovoltaic parameters (PCE, $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ , and FF) for the pristine and HFSTT-modified devices. The average $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ increased from $24.11\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the pristine and the $0.3\mathrm{mol}\%$ -HFSTT-doped devices (target device). A significant improvement in the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF values was observed for the HFSTT-modified device, and the values were higher than the values recorded for the pristine devices. The average $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ increased from 1.135 to $1.181\mathrm{V},$ and the average FF increased from 0.782 to 0.819. The increasement could be attributed to the process of retarded charge-recombination realized in the presence of HFSTT.[62] To gain more insight into the processes of charge transport and recombination, we measured the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ values as functions of light intensity (Figure 3b). A linear relationship between $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and the logarithm of light intensity was observed. Shockley–Read–Hall (SRH) recombination is related to the slope of the light-dependent $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ .[63] The extent of SRH recombination decreases as the slope approaches unity. The HFSTT-based target device is estimated to be 1.17 $k_{\mathrm{B}}T/q$ , which is much lower than the $1.61~k_{\mathrm{B}}T/q$ for the pristine sample $(k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ the temperature in Kelvin, and $\boldsymbol{q}$ the electric charge). This result further proves that the HFSTT-modified PSC device is characterized by a low trap-assisted nonradiative recombination process. Figure  3c presents the external quantum efficiency spectrum obtained under each optimal condition. The results confirm the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values obtained by analyzing the $J{-}V$ curves. The values of the integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ were 23.72 and $24.14~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the pristine and target devices, respectively. Consequently, the maximum PCE realized for the target devices increased significantly from $22.02\%$ to $23.88\%$ under conditions of reverse scan (Figure 3d). The best performance photovoltaic characteristics of the solar cells achieved under various conditions are summarized in Table 1. The hysteretic index $(\mathrm{HI~=~(PCE_{Reverse}–P C E_{F o r w a r d})/}$ $\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{Reverse}})$ was also reduced from $3.5\%$ (pristine device) to $1.1\%$ (target device), implying reduced defects in the HFSTT-doped perovskite films. The reduction can be attributed to the fact that the origin of hysteresis can be attributed to the presence of defects, carrier trapping processes, and capacitive effects.[37,64,65] Considering that the bandgap energy of the perovskite following HFSTT doping is $\approx1.525\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S7, Supporting Information),
$V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ loss is only 0.341 V $(E_{\mathrm{abs}}/q-V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ at the absorption threshold $(E_{\mathrm{abs}})$ . The value is even less than the value recorded for the crystal silicon solar cells (0.38 V) and comparable to the value recorded for the GaAs solar cells (0.30 V). In addition, PSCs that are $1.0\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ in size also exhibit substantial improvements in PCE (increased from $21.17\%$ to $22.52\%$ ) in the presence of HFSTT (Figure 3e, and Table 1).
All those device photovoltaic performance and stability we mentioned above. Notably, our $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ device shows outstanding performance is comparable with the recently published work as we list in Table S2 of the Supporting Information. The charge carrier lifetime and charge carrier extraction properties were analyzed, determining the transient photovoltage and transient photocurrent (TPC) values, respectively, to better understand the device performance enhancement achieved under conditions of HFSTT binding.[66,67] As shown in Figure 3f, the charge-recombination lifetimes $(\tau_{\mathrm{r}})$ of the pristine devices was $9.36~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ . The target device containing HFSTT is characterized by a long decay time of $36.53~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ . This indicates the delay in the charge recombination process, demonstrating good consistency with the improved photovoltaic performance attributable to the surface and grain boundary passivation processes. The TPC was measured under the short-circuit condition to investigate the influence of HFSTT on the charge transfer processes associated with the devices. The charge transfer time $(\tau_{\mathrm{t}})$ of the device decreases from $7.78~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ (pristine) and $2.23~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ (target). This indicates that charge injection and migration to electrodes were preferred. Thus, the facilitated charge transfer process in the target device could be attributed to defect annihilation (Figure 3g). A steady-state PCE was also investigated at the maximum power point under continuous light soaking conditions in ambient air for a time period of $>3600$ s (without encapsulation; RH maintained approximately in the range of $5\mathrm{-}10\%$ ; temperature controlled at ${\approx}45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ). The PCE of the pristine device decreased from $21.62\%$ to $18.45\%$ , while the PCE of the HFSTTbased device decreased from $23.72\%$ to $23.49\%$ (Figure  3h). The suppression of ion migration at the grain boundary and defect passivation in the perovskite film modified by HFSTT were responsible for the significantly improved stability.[68,69]
These results reveal that the incorporation of HFSTT significantly improves the long-term operation stability and efficiency of PSCs. The XRD technique was used to study the stability of the perovskite film (Figure S8, Supporting Information). It was observed that the HFSTT-modified perovskite film was more stable in the presence of moisture and thermal treatment conditions than the pristine film. We monitored the change in PCE by conducting damp heat tests (without any encapsulation) under conditions of $60~\%$ RH in ambient air to further confirm the enhancement in environmental stability of the solar cell devices. Poly[bis (4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl) amine] was used to instead Spiro-OMeTAD as a hole transport layer. Figure  3i presents the device performance recorded for the pristine and HFSTT-based target devices. Stability tracking experiments were performed after aging the samples for $1000\mathrm{h}$ . It was observed that the target devices retained $\approx70\%$ of their initial PCE under these conditions. However, only $\approx40\%$ (aging time: ${\approx}250\mathrm{~k~}$ ) of the initial PCE was retained by the pristine devices. Correspondingly, as shown in the inset of Figure  3i (pristine device), the gold electrode diffused into the degraded perovskite layers (when the samples were not subjected to aging conditions). Gold diffusion was not observed in the system modified using HFSTT. This indicated the high stability of the HFSTT modified perovskite films. Moreover, the $\mathrm{\DeltaT_{80}}$ parameter recorded for the HFSTT-based devices was approximately ten times higher than the $\mathrm{T}_{80}$ parameter recorded for the pristine samples $\mathrm{\DeltaT_{80}}$ indicates the time taken for a solar cell to lose its PCE by $20\%$ (from its initial PCE)). The process of slow degradation can be ascribed to the presence of the fluorinated long alkyl chains (hydrophobic tails) anchoring on the perovskite surface and passivated grain boundaries. In addition, the improvement in the stability could also be attributed to strong hydrogen bonding $(\mathrm{F\mathrm{-}H})$ and chelating (formation of chelates between $\mathrm{F-Pb}$ and ${\mathrm{S}}{=}{\mathrm{O}}{-}{\mathrm{Pb}}$ ) effects.[68,70–72]
We calculated the work function, valence band maximum (VBM), and conduction band minimum (CBM) levels for the pristine and HFSTT-modified perovskite films using the ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS, Figure 4a,b) technique to understand the reason behind the increase in the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ values of the solar cells. Figure 4c presents the energy level diagrams generated based on the data obtained using the UPS and optical absorption techniques. The VBM was located at 1.23 and $1.38~\mathrm{eV}$ below the Fermi level $(E_{\mathrm{F}})$ for the pristine films and HFSTT-modified films, respectively. The work function was determined from the cut-off region, and it changed from 4.45 to $4.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ following HFSTT modification. The lowering in the value could be ascribed to the generation of the interfacial dipole on the upper surface of the perovskite. $[73-75]$ The control and optimized perovskite films were n-type semiconductors as the Fermi levels were close to the conduction bands. It was observed that the work function shifted by ${\approx}280\ \mathrm{meV}$ toward the vacuum level $(E_{\mathrm{VAC}})$ , and the $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ shifted by $150~\mathrm{meV}$ toward CBM, indicating the n-type nature for the HFSTT-modified perovskite film. Therefore, the built-in electric field between the n-type perovskite and p-type Spiro-OMeTAD increases under these conditions, resulting in an increase in the strength of the driving force inducing the separation of the photogenerated carriers and generating high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ for HFSTT-modified PSCs.[76–78] The results agree well with the results obtained by analyzing the TPC data, which revealed that less amount of charge is accumulated at the perovskite and Spiro-OMeTAD interface in the HFSTT-modified perovskite devices (Figure  4d; Figure S9, Supporting Information). We plotted the capacitance–voltage (C–V) curve (Figure 4e) and confirmed that the built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of the pristine device increased significantly (from 1.09 to 1.18  V) when the system was modified using HFSTT ( $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ is obtained from the Mott–Schottky equation: 12 = 2(2Vbi − v) , where A represents the device area, $N_{\mathrm{A}}$ denotes the carrier concentration, $V$ represents the applied bias, $C$ indicates the capacitance, $\ensuremath{\varepsilon}_{0}$ is the vacuum permittivity, and $\varepsilon$ is the relative dielectric constant of the perovskite).[79,80]
The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy technique was used to obtain insights into the process of charge carrier recombination occurring in the PSCs. The corresponding Nyquist plots and equivalent circuit model are presented in Figure 4f (the fitting parameters are listed in Table S3, Supporting Information). We found that the HFSTT-modified devices exhibited low series resistance $(R_{\mathrm{s}})$ and significantly high recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ . The values were higher than the corresponding values of the pristine device. The low $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ recorded for the target device, associated with enhanced charge transfer, results in the generation of high FF of the target device. The high $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ value indicates that the rate of recombination at the interface is low.[81] The space chargelimited current (SCLC) technique was used to accurately quantify the trap density $(n_{\mathrm{trap}})$ .[82] The value of $n_{\mathrm{trap}}$ was determined from the trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ $\lvert V_{\mathrm{TFL}}\rvert=e n_{\mathrm{trap}}L^{2}/(2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{\mathrm{0}})$ , where $L$ is the thickness of the perovskite film, $\varepsilon$ and $\varepsilon_{0}$ are the relative dielectric constants of perovskite and vacuum permittivity, respectively, and $e$ is the elementary charge) (Figure  4g,h). The electron and hole trap densities for the HFSTT-modified perovskite were much lower than the corresponding values recorded for the pristine case. The reduction in the trap density and the improvement in the chare carrier property were in good agreement with the results presented in the previous sections, where it has been presented that the surface and grain boundary defects were well annihilated by the introduction of HFSTT. The corresponding parameters and calculation results have been summarized in Table S4 of the Supporting Information.
Fabrication of a uniform and pin-hole-free large-area perovskite film is a prerequisite for realizing high-efficiency perovskite modules. We evaluated the perovskite film uniformity by fabricating a $10\times10~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ perovskite film. We determined the PCE by analyzing the divided $2.5\times2.5~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ device (Figure S10, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 5a, the device performance was measured using a $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ mask. For the HFSTT-modified device, the average efficiency of the 16 divided devices was $22.31\%$ , which was comparable to the value recorded for the unit $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ device. The photovoltaic parameters (PCE, $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ FF, and $V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ did not vary significantly when the location distribution was changed (the corresponding photovoltaic characteristics are summarized in Table S5, Supporting Information). The PCE distribution recorded for the pristine sample exhibits the local difference phenomenon (the device PCE at the center was significantly higher than the device PCE at the edge). The low-efficiency cells present near the edge are characterized by FF and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ values. This can be attributed to the poor uniformity of the perovskite film.
We used a series of monolithic connection modules to investigate the device performance in the presence of HFSTT.
The details regarding the design of PSCMs are presented in Figure S11 of the Supporting Information. Figure  5c presents the optical microscopic image of the etched P1, P2, and P3 samples. The etching process was conducted for a series of interconnections between the cells. The corresponding GFF was $92.5\%$ . The PSCMs based on the HFSTT-added perovskite materials exhibited the best photovoltaic performance (PCE aperture area: $9.47\%$ . The corresponding active area $(25.98\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ 1 efficiency was recorded to be $21.05\%$ . However, the most efficient PSCM was prepared from the pristine sample $(17.37\%$ ; active area PCE: $18.78\%$ . The average voltage contribution of each subcell $(V_{\mathrm{oc\cdotSub)}}$ in the target HFSTT-added module was ${\approx}1.182\mathrm{~V~}$ ( $V_{\mathrm{oc-Sub}}=$ Total $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of module/subcell number). The value was comparable to the value obtained for the unit small cell $(0.14~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ . This indicated that there was almost no voltage loss during the size upscaling process. The photovoltaic parameters of the PSCMs fabricated using different perovskite films are summarized in Table 2, and the data obtained from the statistical analyses regarding the PSCM performance of the systems based on the 25-piece PSCMs are presented in Figure S12 of the Supporting Information. The results are comparable to currently published work (Table S6, Supporting Information). The corresponding steady PCE at the maximum power condition was tested under light soaking conditions in ambient air (RH maintained in the range of $5-10\%$ ; Figure  5e). A large decrease in PCE was observed for the pristine device.
The optimal target module was characterized by a stabilized active area PCE of ${\approx}20.4\%$ (aperture area: $18.76\%$ ) over a period of 4000 s-tracking at a bias voltage of $8.05\mathrm{~V}.$ We fabricated PSCMs with 12 subcells in series on a $100\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ FTO substrate to further verify the upscalability of our module coating strategy (Figure S13, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure S6f of the Supporting Information, the optimal PCE of $18.27\%$ was achieved when the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ value was 14.148  V, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value was $1.671\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and the FF value was 0.773 for an active area of $60.68\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (forward scan: $16.91\%$ ; $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ : 13.701 V; $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ $1.666\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ ; FF: 0.741). Considering that the GFF of the PSCMs was $92.5\%$ , the aperture area PCE reached a maximum of $16.90\%$ (forward scan: $15.64\%$ ). It is noteworthy that the HFSTT-added target module fabricated by us exhibited excellent operational stability under continuous 1 sun illumination conditions (temperature: ${\approx}45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . The target module maintained $80\%$ of its initial efficiency after $1800\mathrm{~h~}$ of operation. This was significantly higher than the $\tau_{80}$ value recorded for the pristine device operated for ${\approx}300\mathrm{~h~}$ . The origin of the enhanced stability could be attributed to the strong interactions present between the HFSTT unit and the perovskite materials. Under these conditions, the passivation of the grain boundary and surface of the perovskite can be realized. The corresponding cross-sectional SEM images of the lightexposed aged devices containing or devoid of HFSTT additives are shown in Figure S14 of the Supporting Information. A significant change in the compact perovskite in the HFSTT-incorporated device was not observed, whereas the pristine perovskite degraded, resulting in the generation of a messy morphology. We present a new process to fabricate a highly efficient integrated system to realize solar water splitting at an affordable cost using NiCoS nanoclusters as electrocatalysts and monolithic PSCM (Video S1, Supporting Information) to evaluate the performance of the solar module under outdoor conditions. The developed method can potentially help to bring the solar and hydrogen economy closer to ambient reality conditions.
## 3. Conclusion
We successfully developed a multifunctional sulfonate surfactant anchoring strategy in the perovskite precursor solution which to fabricate a high-quality compact (pin-hole free) film characterized by a large area. The sulfonate surfactant, HFSTT incorporated in perovskite precursor solution was revealed to work as a surface tension regulator and defect passivator, as well as a perovskite structure stabilizer. The addition of HFSTT helped improve the wetting property of the perovskite solution. Thus, a large and uniform coating of the perovskite layer was realized for the module device. The HFSTT unit containing fluorinated long alkyl chains and functionalized sulfonate terminals can strongly passivate charged defects and chelate with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ cations. The HFSTT units helped passivate the grain boundaries and surfaces of the perovskite materials, resulting in improved stability under conditions of moisture and ion migration. The incorporation of HFSTT resulted in the generation of high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ values of $1.184\mathrm{~V~}$ (voltage deficit: 0.341  V). The maximum PCE recorded was $23.88\%$ , and the steady-state output efficiency was $23.72\%$ for the target PSCs. The unencapsulated target devices retained $\approx70\%$ of their initial efficiencies after $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ of heat damping tests $(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C};\approx60\%$ RH). Furthermore, the strategy has been successfully extended to the fabrication of PSCMs that exhibit excellent photovoltaic performance with PCEs of $21.05\%$ $(25.98\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and $18.27\%$ $(60.68\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ under conditions of reverse scan. We believe that the developed multifunctional sulfonate surfactant anchoring strategy can be potentially used to develop highly efficient PSC modules. The results presented herein can also help develop new additive designs that can be adopted to improve the efficiency and stability of the devices that meet the commercial application standards.
